Subject: [PATCH] [mlir] Walk nested regions from an explicit stack

Every walk of `Visitors.h` and `Visitors.cpp` (over regions, blocks and
operations, void and interruptible, and the two stage-aware walks) calls
itself once per level of operation nesting, as the "This walk should be
iterative over the operations" TODO says. The native stack a pass needs
therefore grows with the depth of the IR it walks, and a deeply nested
function overflows the stack of a thread with a small one.

The walks now share one engine that keeps its position in an explicit
stack, one entry per operation whose walk has begun. Each walk is a
visitor that says where its callback is made, how its result skips or
interrupts the walk, and whether the next block or operation is taken
before the current one is walked, which is what lets a callback erase the
one it is given. Every range is made, every callback made and every
iterator advanced at the point where the recursive walk did it, for every
iterator kind. An entry never moves while it is on the stack, since a
post-order traversal of a region's blocks keeps its state outside its
iterators; the first entries live in the stack itself, so a shallow walk
allocates nothing.

A unit test walks a chain of operations nested 10,000 deep, with every
kind of walk, on a thread whose stack a frame per level would overflow.
